      "text": "To determine the final price of the shirt after applying the two successive discounts, we start by calculating the price of the shirt after the first discount of 20% off.\n\nThe regular price of the shirt is $30. A 20% discount means the selling price is 80% of the original price:\n\\[\n\\text{Price after first discount} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.20) = 30 \\times 0.80 = 24\n\\]\nNow, Mary uses her coupon which takes another 25% off the original price of $30. Therefore, the new price Mary pays after applying the coupon is:\n\\[\n\\text{Final price} = 30 \\times (1 - 0.25) = 30 \\times 0.75 = 22.50\n\\]\nNext, we need to verify that the final price of \\( \\$22.50 \\) is equivalent to a single discount. To do this, let's denote the single discount as \\( x\\% \\). The final price obtained can be represented by:\n\\[\n\\text{Final price} = 30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100})\n\\]\nWe know this final price equals \\( \\$22.50 \\):\n\\[\n30 \\times (1 - \\frac{x}{100}) = 22.50\n\\]\nSolving for \\( x \\):\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{x}{100} = \\frac{22.50}{30}\n\\]\n\\[\n1 - \\frac{x}{100} = 0.75\n\\]\n\\[\n1 - 0.75 = \\frac{x}{100}\n\\]\n\\[\n0.25 = \\frac{x}{100}\n\\]\n\\[\nx = 0.25 \\times 100 = 25 \\%\n\\]\nSo, the final price of \\( \\$22.50 \\) is indeed achieved by applying a single discount of \\( 25\\% \\). Therefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{25}\n\\]",
